# The frame-time table of references/runtime-comparison.md section 5, re-derived
# from the tree instead of from a scratch program. Windows 11 + RTX 3060, ONNX
# Runtime 1.32.0 (API 32), 2026-11-27.
#
# The two commands that produce this file, from a clean checkout:
#
#   python tools/make-testmodels.py --out .validation/testmodels
#   tools\bench-ep-frame.bat .validation\ort-gpu-1.32.0 \
#       .validation\testmodels\conv_chain_3ch_64.onnx 1920 1080
#
# The model is now generated by a tool in this repository (conv_chain_model in
# tools/make-testmodels.py: three 3x3 convs, RGB to 64 channels, H and W dynamic),
# where the original table's 1080p rows came from a .validation/big_conv.onnx that
# only existed on this machine. The probe is tools/bench-ep-frame.cpp, built into
# the package's bin directory by the script so that the onnxruntime.dll and
# onnxruntime_providers_cuda.dll it loads are the ones a bundle ships.
#
# What the numbers are: one Run() per frame - an OrtValue over the caller's host
# buffer, Run, memcpy back, the same per-frame path ModelRunner::Run takes - with
# default options (10 iterations, 3 warmup, 1 intra-op thread) and two rounds in
# opposite order. The first frame of each provider is printed before the measured
# ones because it carries context creation and kernel loading.
#
# Reproduced against the table's row: CUDA 111.6 / 114.0 ms median here against
# 111.7 / 112.4 ms, CPU 3,036 / 3,058 ms against 2,990 / 3,025 ms - the same
# frame, the same provider options, the same machine, minutes apart in code and
# months apart in nothing. A different graph would have moved the GPU row by more
# than 2 %, which is the check that makes this a reproduction rather than a
# coincidence.
model: .validation\testmodels\conv_chain_3ch_64.onnx  1920x1080, 3 channel(s), 6220800 values per frame
runtime: ONNX Runtime 1.32.0, API 32, linking the onnxruntime.dll beside this exe
intra-op threads 1, ORT_ENABLE_ALL, per-frame OrtValue over the host buffer
CPU first frame (untimed run): 3451.98 ms
CUDA first frame (untimed run): 381.51 ms
== round 1: CPU then CUDA ==
  min 2946.95  median 3035.86  p90 3058.81  max 3070.26  mean 3011.32  (0.33 fps at the median)
  min 109.60  median 111.63  p90 114.39  max 116.15  mean 112.37  (8.96 fps at the median)
== round 2: CUDA then CPU ==
  min 110.37  median 113.96  p90 119.30  max 123.46  mean 114.18  (8.77 fps at the median)
  min 2993.53  median 3058.17  p90 3163.61  max 3213.06  mean 3082.08  (0.33 fps at the median)
